---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import ConfidenceBadge from '../components/ConfidenceBadge.astro';
import FaqBlock from '../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../site';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How is a freight rate figure sourced?',
    a: 'A rate is first pulled from a primary source — a carrier tariff, a port authority publication or a customs document. If no primary source exists, the figure is marked LOW confidence and labelled "verify before relying". A figure is never invented to fill a gap.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does a LOW confidence label mean?',
    a: 'It means the figure is an unverified estimate — often because the underlying rate or rule is not publicly published, or because it changes too quickly to pin down. Treat LOW-confidence figures as a starting point and confirm them with a carrier or authority before relying on them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How often is the data updated?',
    a: 'Each page carries a last-updated marker, and data is refreshed when a primary source changes — for example, a new VAT rate or a revised customs rule. Rates and transit times, which move constantly, are deliberately labelled as estimates rather than fixed values.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do you verify a freight provider in the directory?',
    a: 'A provider is marked "verified" only after its business identity, contact details and service claims are independently confirmed against a primary source — an official licence (NVOCC, FMC or equivalent), a government registry, or the provider\u2019s own published credentials. Until then it stays "unverified".',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'FAQPage',
    data: {
      mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How WorldFreightHub sources and verifies freight data, assigns confidence levels, and keeps every figure traceable to a primary source."
  canonical={`${SITE_URL}/methodology/`}
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: 'Methodology' }]} />
      <h1>Methodology</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        How we source, structure and verify the data behind WorldFreightHub.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Primary sources first</h2>
    <p>
      We prefer primary sources: government customs and tax authorities, port
      authorities, and carriers. Secondary or aggregated data is used only to
      cross-check, never as the sole basis for a published figure. The source
      hierarchy is:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Government / port authority</strong> — customs and tax
        authorities (ZATCA, Dubai Customs, Qatar Customs, and so on), port
        authorities (Mawani, DP World, SIPG), and official notices.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Carrier / operator</strong> — published tariffs, schedules and
        service guides from shipping lines and terminal operators.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Industry / organisation</strong> — trade bodies, standards
        organisations (ICC, IATA, FIATA) and recognised industry references.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Where a fact comes from a marketplace listing, a competitor page or an
      unattributed source, it is labelled accordingly and treated as
      unverified.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Confidence levels</h2>
    <p>
      Every estimate carries a confidence level so readers know how much weight
      to give it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /> — confirmed against a primary source.
        Example: a VAT rate published by a national tax authority.
      </li>
      <li>
        <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /> — corroborated by multiple secondary
        sources, or a primary source that is a snapshot rather than a live
        figure. Example: a freight-rate range assembled from several carrier
        references.
      </li>
      <li>
        <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /> — an unverified estimate with no
        published primary source. Example: a port's free-time allowance that
        varies by terminal and is not publicly posted.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A LOW label is not a failure — it is an honest statement that the figure
      could not be pinned to a source, and that you should confirm it before
      relying on it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Rates and transit times</h2>
      <p>
        Freight rates and transit times fluctuate with fuel, season and
        geopolitical risk, so they are published as <strong>indicative
        estimates with a confidence level</strong>, never as live quotes. A rate
        range such as "20ft FCL: $1,500–$6,200" is a snapshot of the plausible
        spread, not a commitment — the wide range on Gulf lanes is itself real
        information, reflecting how much war-risk premiums and demand move the
        market.
      </p>
      <p>
        No live pricing is shown anywhere on the site. When you need a firm
        number, the get-quote form routes your request to a provider who can
        quote your specific shipment.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Verification workflow</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Collect.</strong> A fact is pulled from the highest-quality
        source available, with the source recorded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Label.</strong> The figure is assigned a confidence level based
        on how it was sourced — HIGH for primary confirmation, LOW for an
        unverified estimate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cross-check.</strong> Figures that can be corroborated by an
        independent primary source are upgraded; anything that cannot be
        verified stays marked as an estimate.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Publish or withhold.</strong> A figure is published only if it
        is sourced or clearly labelled. If it can be neither sourced nor
        labelled honestly, it is withheld rather than invented.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The same discipline applies to the provider directory: a listing is
      marked "verified" only after its identity and contact details are
      independently confirmed, and everything self-reported is labelled as
      such.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <FaqBlock items={faqs} />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
